<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">First Italo-Ethiopian War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1895">1895</a>–<a href="/years/1896">1896</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The First Italo-Ethiopian War was fought in 1895–1896 between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopian-empire">Ethiopian Empire</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menelik-ii">Menelik II</a>. It ended with the crushing Italian defeat at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-adwa">Battle of Adwa</a> in 1896, which secured Ethiopian independence and was a major humiliation for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-empire">Italian Empire</a>'s colonial ambitions.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/first-italo-ethiopian-war.jpg" alt="First Italo-Ethiopian War" width="320" height="364" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Le Petit Journal - F. Méaulle, scan by Vob08; derivative work by Zheim · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Menelik_-_Adoua-2_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Italian troops landed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/massawa">Massawa</a> on 5 February 1885 to replace the departing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egyptians">Egyptians</a>. After an Ethiopian force under Ras Alula destroyed an Italian column at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-dogali">Battle of Dogali</a> in 1887, the Italians held back for a time. On 2 May 1889 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menelik-ii">Menelik II</a>, newly proclaimed emperor, signed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-wuchale">Treaty of Wuchale</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. The Italian and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amharic">Amharic</a> texts differed: the Italian version made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a> conduct its foreign affairs through Italy, effectively a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a>, while the Amharic version did not.</p><p>Menelik learned of the Italian reading in 1890, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/queen-victoria">Queen Victoria</a>'s reply told him that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> could not deal with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a> directly. In 1893 he repudiated the treaty. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, led by Prime Minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francesco-crispi">Francesco Crispi</a>, who sought a second Roman empire, turned to force. Menelik meanwhile imported large numbers of rifles and artillery from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and others.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>In December 1894 the Italians crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mareb-river">Mareb River</a> into Tigray. General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oreste-baratieri">Oreste Baratieri</a> defeated Ras Mengesha Yohannes at Coatit in January 1895 and again at Senafe, then occupied Adigrat and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-adwa">Adwa</a>. On 17 September 1895 Menelik called his men to arms, and leaders of every region answered, assembling an army of over 100,000. On 7 December 1895 Ras Makonnen overran the Italian position at Amba Alagi, killing Major Pietro Toselli.</p><p>Menelik and Empress Taytu Betul joined the siege of the fort at Mekele, which surrendered on 21 January 1896 after its water was cut off; the garrison was allowed to leave. On 1 March 1896 at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-adwa">Adwa</a> three Italian brigades, separated on mountain tracks, met an Ethiopian army of between 73,000 and 120,000. By noon they were in retreat. Estimates put Italian dead at between 5,900 and 6,133, with thousands captured; the Italians left behind their artillery.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>News of the defeat brought riots in Italian cities, and within two weeks Crispi was forced to resign. Talks failed when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> would accept the end of its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> claim only if <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a> took no protection from another power. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-addis-ababa">Treaty of Addis Ababa</a> of October 1896 set the borders of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eritrea">Eritrea</a> and obliged Italy to recognise Ethiopia's independence. Captured Eritrean askaris, regarded as traitors, had their right hands and left feet amputated; Italian prisoners were generally treated better.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> sent missions to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/addis-ababa">Addis Ababa</a>, and France signed a treaty in March 1897. As one of the first decisive victories of an African army over a European colonial power, the war became a symbol of pan-Africanism. Ethiopian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> lasted until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-italo-ethiopian-war">Second Italo-Ethiopian War</a> of 1935–36.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “First Italo-Ethiopian War” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Early colonial campaigns revealed significant weaknesses in Italian military organisation, most plainly in the <b>First Italo-Ethiopian War</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/italian-empire">Italian Empire · Military</a></li></ul></section>
